# Fleet charter — seed

Copy this to `missions/fleet.md` beside your `fleet.yaml`, fill it in, and
point at it from the manifest:

```yaml
fleet:
  mission: >
    One paragraph. EVERY bot receives this.
  mission_file: missions/fleet.md   # this file; composed for managers only
```

`mission:` is the one-paragraph anchor every bot gets. This file is the fuller
charter, composed into a manager's instructions and read before it picks work.
`mission_file` requires `mission` — the validator enforces it.

## What this fleet is for

State the outcome, not the activity. "Ship and operate the storefront" is a
mission; "write Python" is not. A manager weighs candidate work against this
sentence, so it has to be able to rule something OUT.

## What is in scope

Name the projects and the repos. These should line up with `projects.yaml`
(or, if you have not written one, with the `scope.repos` your bots declare —
that is what the derived Projects table is built from).

## What is out of scope

The most useful section, and the one most often left blank. A fleet with no
stated boundary drifts into whatever arrived most recently.

## How work closes here

Per-project closure bars live in `projects.yaml` as validation tiers. Use this
section for anything that applies fleet-wide — a release window, an approval
that is always required, a thing that must never be automated.
